[s0l0!-header][h1]Passing by Value[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

By default, arguments in C++ are passed [b]by value[/b].
When passed by value, a copy of the argument is passed to the function.

[s0l0!-header][b]Example: [/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="cpp" codeId="250"]void myFunc(int x) {
  x = 100;
}

int main() {
  int var = 20;
  myFunc(var);
  cout << var;
}
[/code]
[note]Because a copy of the argument is passed to the function, the original argument is not modified by the function.[/note]

